The Autostart mechanism is a specification of freedesktop.org, implemented by
many Linux desktops, including the Raspberry Pi's.

The files in this directory allow various DerbyNet-related applications to be
automatically started by copying the corresponding desktop file to the user's
.config/autostart folder, e.g., to /home/pi/.config/autostart/.  (It may be
necessary first to create the .config/autostart/ folder.)

It's unlikely that you would want to autostart ALL the files in this folder.


A kiosk, track camera or photo stand on a Raspberry Pi
------------------------------------------------------

For a Pi 4 or 5 with current Raspberry Pi OS (with desktop):

1. Install Chromium if it isn't there:  sudo apt install chromium
   (On older releases the package is chromium-browser.)

2. In derbynet.conf -- /boot/firmware/derbynet.conf, which you can also edit
   from another computer with the SD card -- set the server:

       DERBYNET_SERVER="http://192.168.1.100/derbynet"

   For a track camera rather than a kiosk, also (any name: Start, Finish...):

       KIOSK_PAGE="camera.php?name=Finish"

   For a photo stand (a camera and a USB barcode scanner taking racer or
   car photos), also:

       KIOSK_PAGE=photo-stand.php

   A photo stand asks for a login the first time, and again after the Pi
   restarts: choose Photo (password flashbulb, unless changed).

   (If DerbyNet's extras package isn't installed, create the file.)

3. Turn off screen blanking:  sudo raspi-config, Display Options, Screen
   Blanking, No.

4. Start it with the desktop:

       mkdir -p ~/.config/autostart
       cp /usr/share/derbynet/autostart/kiosk.desktop ~/.config/autostart/

   (kiosk.desktop runs /usr/share/derbynet/scripts/kiosk.sh; if the script is
   somewhere else, edit its Exec line.)

5. Reboot.  The Pi shows "Waiting for the DerbyNet server" until it can
   reach it, then goes full-screen.  A kiosk appears on the Kiosk Dashboard
   under its serial number (or ADDRESS, if set in derbynet.conf), where you
   choose what it shows; it keeps that name across restarts.

To get out of full-screen on the Pi: Alt+F4 closes the browser (it restarts
by itself after a few seconds); to stop it for good, remove the file from
~/.config/autostart and reboot.
